<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!--
      The launcher's window **shows the system wallpaper**, which is what makes it a launcher's window rather than an
      app's that happens to draw a home screen.

      The three items are the platform's own `Theme.Wallpaper` recipe rather than an invention: `windowShowWallpaper`
      asks the window manager to composite the wallpaper behind this window, and it can only show through if the
      window's own background is transparent — `colorBackgroundCacheHint` is null for the same reason, since a cached
      opaque background would defeat both.

      **Three things were waiting on this and none of them could be faked.** Capture (S5d) takes a screenshot of the
      wallpaper with the launcher hidden, and without this it photographed this app's own background. The live icon
      preview in every settings section punches through to the wallpaper with `BlendMode.Src`, which needs something
      behind it to punch to. And the folder's frosted backdrop (S5f) is a blur *of* the wallpaper. L1's launcher window
      did this from the start; L2 deferred it while there was nothing yet to see.

      Still `Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar`'s child: nothing about the palette changes here, because every surface
      themes itself through `LauncherTheme`. This decides only what is *behind* them.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.MorphicLauncher2" parent="android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowShowWallpaper">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:colorBackgroundCacheHint">@null</item>
    </style>

</resources>
